{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_viiu6jkocy3ugpjz67kq2gzthmkbfwemk5c35zlb6tlqq4ciowpa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01DGJhW3RbgkJSXinZ3R5FTy","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Independent C-vs-Rust review","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are doing an INDEPENDENT line-by-line correctness review of a C-to-Rust translation. Do NOT modify any files. Report findings only.\n\nFiles (read them fully):\n- `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-9oPgJg/c_src/include/lib.h`\n- `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-9oPgJg/c_src/src/lib.c`  (645 lines, the ground truth)\n- `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-9oPgJg/translation/src/lib.rs` (910 lines, the translation)\n\nThe C is ALWAYS correct. The Rust must reproduce it exactly, including quirks and bugs.\n\nYour job: for EVERY one of the 39 non-static C functions, compare the C body statement-by-statement against the corresponding Rust function and report ANY semantic difference, however small. Pay particular attention to:\n\n1. Comparison operators: `<` vs `<=`, `>` vs `>=`, and which side of a `&&`/`||` they are on.\n2. Operand ORDER in every arithmetic expression (`a.x*b.y - a.y*b.x` vs the reverse), and in every function call argument list.\n3. Which struct field is assigned from which (e.g. `out[1] = c2V(bb->max.x, bb->min.y)`).\n4. `switch` statements: exactly which `case` labels exist, whether there is a `default:`, and whether any case FALLS THROUGH into another. Check that the Rust `match` arms cover exactly the same value sets, and that a missing C `default:` is translated as \"do nothing\" and not as something else.\n5. Aliasing/ordering of struct assignments like `s->b = s->a; s->a = s->c;` — order matters.\n6. The array-vs-named-field modelling: the C has `typedef struct { c2sv a, b, c, d; float div; int count; } c2Simplex;` and does `c2sv *verts = &s.a;`. The Rust models it as `verts: [c2sv; 4]`. Verify that EVERY use of `s->a`/`s->b`/`s->c` maps to `verts[0]`/`verts[1]`/`verts[2]` correctly in every single function.\n7. Float literal values (e.g. `-1.0e8f`, `1.19209289550781250000000000000000000e-7F`, `3.40282346638528859811704183484516925e+38F`, `2.0f`, `0.5f`) — check the Rust constants are bit-identical.\n8. Integer/enum values: `C2_TYPE_CAPSULE=0, C2_TYPE_CIRCLE=1, C2_TYPE_AABB=2` (note CAPSULE is 0, not CIRCLE) — verify the Rust constants and every `match` on them use the right numbers.\n9. `malloc` sizes in `ptr_from_parts`.\n10. Loop bounds and the direction/initial values of loop counters.\n11. In `c2GJK`: the exact sequence of the cache-read block, the main while loop, the witness/radius block and the cache-write block; the `save_count`/`saveA`/`saveB` handling; the `dup` detection; where `++s.count` and `++iter` happen relative to the `break`s.\n\nAlso: run `grep -nE '^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_ *]*\\(' /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-9oPgJg/c_src/src/lib.c` (or similar) to enumerate every function DEFINITION in the C file, and confirm there are exactly 39 and that each has a Rust counterpart with a `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub extern \"C\"` wrapper using the same symbol name. Report any C function with no Rust counterpart, or any Rust function whose signature (argument types/order, by-value vs by-pointer, return type) differs from the C.\n\nOutput format:\n- A numbered list of ANY discrepancies found, each with: C line number(s), the C code, the Rust line number(s), the Rust code, and a one-line explanation of the behavioural difference.\n- If a function is a faithful match, do NOT list it. Only report problems.\n- End with an explicit line: \"TOTAL DISCREPANCIES: N\" and, separately, \"C FUNCTION DEFINITIONS FOUND: M\".\n\nBe extremely literal and pedantic. Do not assume the translation is correct; verify each expression character by character."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"us