{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_snxxivcun3cgj2zas4vyhtrnqi2hl4463vqi6zxgl2pn4dgznrjq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. The independent review agent found **0 discrepancies across all 39 functions**, and I verified its three substantive claims myself rather than taking them on faith:\n\n| Agent claim | My independent check | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `c2Simplex` offsets 0/36/72/108/144/148, size 152 | compiled my own `offsetof` probe | ✅ exact match — the Rust `verts: [c2sv; 4]` model is correct |\n| `lib.rs` unmodified by the mutation run | `diff` vs pre-mutation backup | ✅ byte-identical |\n| ~99.8% line / 98.7% branch-arc C coverage | reproduced gcov myself | ✅ **better** — see below |\n\nReproducing the coverage myself was worth it, because my first attempt produced *garbage* (`call 0 returned -12003%`, six lines falsely flagged uncovered). Cause: gcov's default counters aren't atomic and cargo runs tests in parallel. With `-fprofile-update=atomic` and `--test-threads=1`:\n\n```\nLines executed:       100.00% of 445\nBranches executed:    100.00% of 157\nTaken at least once:   99.36% of 157\nCalls executed:       100.00% of 149\nuncovered lines: 0    branch arcs never taken: 1\n```\n\n**The single untaken branch arc in the entire C library is `while (iter < 20)` branch 1** — the loop-cap exit. That is exactly `ERRORS.md` row 19, which I had already independently proven structurally unreachable via 2M randomized calls (max 5 iterations, because a proxy holds ≤4 vertices). Two unrelated methods converging on the same lone dead branch is strong evidence the surface map is complete. Everything else is entered, including all six `c23` region tests, all four `c2AABBtoAABB` separating axes, every `switch` `default:`, and the fall-off-the-end path at `lib.c:637` (hit 19,717 times).\n\nAs a bonus, all 111 tests also pass against that instrumented library — a **second, independently compiled C build** (`gcc -O0 --coverage`, no CMake), so the agreement isn't an artifact of one particular C compilation.\n\n**Final state**\n\n- **Phase A**: `SYMBOLS.md` (39/39, 0 missing/extra), `ERRORS.md` (67 rows, all `[x]`), `CONFIGS.md` (71 rows, all `[x]`); plus `MUTATION.md` and `verify.sh`.\n- **Phases B–D**: 111 tests, 7 binaries, dlopen-only, passing against both the debug and release Rust `.so`, under all three feature spellings, across 16 `SEED_OFFSET` values.\n- **`c_src/` untouched** (md5s unchanged; only `c_src/build/` created). Scratch tooling lives in `work/`, outside the crate.\n\n**The translation required no behavioural changes** — `src/lib.rs` is byte-identical to what I started with. My edits were confined to `Cargo.toml` (`libloading` dev-dep), `.cargo/config.toml` (offline, since crates.io is unreachable), and new test/doc files.\n\nTwo honest caveats I chose to document rather than paper over: three of ~700k float comparisons differ in the NaN **sign bit** only (unspecified SSE operand forwarding — inspectable via `STRICT_NAN_BITS=1`), and five `ERRORS.md` rows are C-side UB with no defined result to match (NULL deref, falling off a non-`void` function, and reads of the C's uninitialised automatic `c2Proxy`). The last of those is where my one apparent \"divergence\" came from, and it was a defect in my *test*, not in the translation."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":880,"cache_read_input_tokens":336655,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":880,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d28431be-c36f-4316-bc23-1df19b169f26","uuid":"ddc46807-e8c9-483f-a130-e97c1b5ad869","timestamp":"2026-08-27T12:39:07.146Z"}
